  "intro": [
    {
      "title": "What the accusative does",
      "title_pl": "Co robi biernik",
      "body": "The English name accusative comes from Latin ideas of “charging / pointing at” something. The Polish name biernik is linked to brać (to take): the form of the thing you take, have, want, or “aim the verb at”.\n\nIn plain terms: the accusative marks the object — what the action is directed at.",
      "body_pl": "Angielskie accusative ≈ „wskazać / oskarżyć o”. Polskie biernik łączy się z brać: forma tego, co bierzemy, mamy, chcemy.\n\nProsto: biernik to dopełnienie — to, na co skierowane jest działanie."
    },
    {
      "title": "Subject and object",
      "title_pl": "Podmiot i dopełnienie",
      "body": "Subject = who does the action (often nominative — mianownik).\nObject = what they act on (often accusative — biernik).\n\nEnglish: I (subject) have coffee (object).\nPolish: Ja mam kawę. — ja is the haver; kawę is what is had.",
      "body_pl": "Podmiot = kto działa (często mianownik).\nDopełnienie = na czym działa (często biernik).\n\nJa mam kawę. — ja ma; kawę jest „brana” przez mieć.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "I have coffee.",
          "pl": "Mam kawę."
        },
        {
          "en": "She has a book.",
          "pl": "Ona ma książkę."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Mieć — the verb forms",
      "title_pl": "Mieć — formy",
      "body": "First lock the person forms of mieć. Then put the object in the accusative after them.",
      "body_pl": "Najpierw formy mieć, potem dopełnienie w bierniku.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Person",
          "Polish",
          "English"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "ja",
            "mam",
            "I have"
          ],
          [
            "ty",
            "masz",
            "you have (sg)"
          ],
          [
            "on / ona / ono",
            "ma",
            "he / she / it has"
          ],
          [
            "my",
            "mamy",
            "we have"
          ],
          [
            "wy",
            "macie",
            "you have (pl)"
          ],
          [
            "oni / one",
            "mają",
            "they have"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Accusative by gender (A1 light)",
      "title_pl": "Biernik według rodzaju",
      "body": "The object form depends on the noun’s gender (and a bit more later). A1 pattern you need now:",
      "body_pl": "Forma dopełnienia zależy od rodzaju. Na A1:",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Gender",
          "Nominative (name it)",
          "Accusative (have it)",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "masculine (thing)",
            "dom, sok, chleb",
            "often same as nom",
            "mam dom · mam sok"
          ],
          [
            "masculine (person/animal)",
            "kot, brat",
            "often -a",
            "mam kota · mam brata"
          ],
          [
            "feminine (-a)",
            "kawa, książka, siostra",
            "-a → -ę",
            "mam kawę · mam książkę · mam siostrę"
          ],
          [
            "neuter (-o / -e)",
            "piwo, miasto, mleko",
            "often same as nom",
            "mam piwo · mam mleko"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Feminine -ę (the clear change)",
      "title_pl": "Żeńskie -ę (widać zmianę)",
      "body": "Most feminine nouns in -a show the accusative clearly: -a becomes -ę. Drill this first — it is the most visible Acc signal at A1.",
      "body_pl": "Większość żeńskich na -a: -a → -ę. To najczytelniejszy sygnał biernika na A1.",
      "table": {
        "headers": [
          "Nominative",
          "Accusative",
          "With mieć"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "kawa",
            "kawę",
